  "response": "The HTTP layer is blocking the async event loop. That directly explains the pattern: latency collapses under concurrency while CPU stays low because workers are waiting synchronously rather than computing.\n\nRanked by likely contribution:\n\n1. **`get_invoice` — primary suspect** (`api/routes.py:21–23`, `crime_00003`)\n   - `requests.get()` performs synchronous network I/O inside an async handler.\n   - While the upstream responds, every other request assigned to that event loop is stalled.\n   - `open()` adds synchronous filesystem I/O, though it is probably less damaging than the network call.\n   - If this is the common read endpoint, it is the strongest explanation for system-wide p99 degradation.\n\n2. **`export_invoice` — severe when exercised** (`api/routes.py:31–32`, `crime_00004`)\n   - `subprocess.run()` blocks until the child process exits.\n   - `time.sleep()` deliberately freezes the event-loop thread.\n   - This may be even worse per request than `get_invoice`, but its overall contribution depends on export traffic.\n\nRecommended fixes:\n\n- Replace `requests.get()` with an awaited async HTTP client such as `httpx.AsyncClient`, including explicit timeouts.\n- Use async file access or move unavoidable filesystem operations through `asyncio.to_thread()`.\n- Replace `time.sleep()` with `await asyncio.sleep()`.\n- Replace `subprocess.run()` with `asyncio.create_subprocess_exec()` or move export generation to a background job.\n\nThe other seven findings are not credible causes of this load-dependent latency collapse. The import cycle can cause startup failures; the date findings can cause correctness bugs; and the large function, deep imports, and naming drift are maintainability risks. They should not distract from fixing the two blocking async handlers first.",
